WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
Most people think building a business

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.480
from scratch is the only way to do it.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.520
You come up with the idea, you grind

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.440
through the early days with no customers

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.160
and no proof that any of it is going to

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.880
work. But, there's another path that a

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.800
lot of people overlook, and in a lot of

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.920
cases, it's actually the smarter move.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.000
And that is buying a business. Not a

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.320
course, and not a franchise, but an

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.520
actual operating business with existing

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.640
customers, existing cash flow, and a

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.320
reputation that is already built. My

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.360
name's Austin Zaback, and I've spent

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.480
over a decade building businesses from

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.080
the ground up across the real estate and

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.640
pest control industry and several

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.280
others. And the longer that I've been

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.680
doing this, the more that I believe

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.800
buying the right business actually beats

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:43.200
building one from scratch in almost

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.000
every situation, if you know what to

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.880
look for. So, today I'm breaking down

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.400
the five businesses that are worth

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.320
buying, why they work as acquisitions,

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.160
and what you need to know before you

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.680
actually write that check. And if at any

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.560
point you get value from the video, do

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.360
me a huge favor, smash that like button

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.320
and subscribe. It really would help me

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.480
out a ton, and it'll get this video in

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.520
front of more people who need to see.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.239
The first business on today's list is

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.240
pest control. And the pest control

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:10.280
industry does over $26 billion a year in

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.280
the US alone. And it's one of the few

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.400
service businesses where the demand is

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.960
almost entirely non-negotiable. If you

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.840
think about it, people don't really call

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.000
a pest control company because they want

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.800
to. They call somebody because they have

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.200
to. And that is exactly why it makes it

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.200
such a compelling acquisition target.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.040
When you buy an existing pest control

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.360
company or route, you're not buying a

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:32.360
concept. You're buying a customer list

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.560
that pays on a recurring basis, either

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.760
monthly, quarterly, or annually, whether

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.880
or not anything is actively wrong at the

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:41.120
property. And that recurring revenue is

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.880
what separates pest control from most of

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.120
the other service businesses that are

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.080
out there. The cash flow is predictable,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.600
and it starts on day one. Now, when

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.200
you're looking at buying a business,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.720
okay, you're going to hear the term

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.280
EBITDA. Now, EBITDA means earnings

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.000
before interest, taxes, depreciation,

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.960
and amortization. And it sounds

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.800
complicated, but all it really means is

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.520
what does the business actually make

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.120
after you strip out all of the noise?

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.400
It's the clearest picture of how

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.240
profitable the operation actually is.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.479
And in pest control, the margins can be

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.680
very, very strong. A well-run pest

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:15.200
control company can operate at a 20 to

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.959
30% EBITDA margin, meaning for every

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.560
dollar that comes in, you're keeping a

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:21.480
significant portion of actual profit.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.280
Pest control businesses typically sell

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.920
anywhere from a 3.3 to an eight times

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.120
EBITDA depending on the size of the

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.040
operation, the customer retention rate,

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.280
and how systematized the business

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.640
actually is. That range kind of exists

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.120
because a small owner-operated route and

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.920
a fully staffed regional company are two

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.720
very different animals. But once you

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.480
understand that multiple, you can kind

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.080
of quickly figure out how long it's

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.320
going to take you to get your money back

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.040
and start building real returns on top

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.000
of what you paid. So, I actually own

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.440
Nectar Pest Control here in Arizona, and

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.760
I can tell you firsthand that the

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.600
customers who stay on a recurring plan

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.520
are not thinking about canceling. They

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.519
set it and forget it, and that loyalty

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.320
does not disappear when the ownership

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:03.680
changes hands. They're loyal to the

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.640
service, not necessarily the person that

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.760
owns or runs the company. And I want to

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.560
be clear, building from scratch is 100%

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.080
possible. My buddy Cameron built a

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.200
company called Green Mango from the

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.480
ground up here in Arizona. He never made

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.560
a single acquisition, and he recently

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.360
sold that company to private equity. And

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:23.160
at his peak, he had 117 trucks on the

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:25.840
road in one market doing over $2 million

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.360
a month in revenue. But here's for most

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.280
people. Cameron is ultimately an

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.040
anomaly. Building to that level takes

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.480
many, many years, and most people

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.640
starting from zero don't have the runway

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.320
to survive the early stages of a

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.519
business like that. Buying a small

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.200
existing pest control operation gives

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:42.800
you that head start that makes the

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.040
entire journey a little bit more

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.680
manageable. You skip the hardest part,

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.200
and you start with customers that are

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.200
already paying you. So, if I was

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.680
starting over today and I had capital to

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.200
deploy or I had access to capital, a

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.120
pest control route would be one of the

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.440
first places that I would look. And you

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.960
might be watching thinking that you

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.000
don't have the money. Business funding

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.400
is not as complicated as it might seem.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.320
If you want to reach out to somebody

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.760
who's really solid that I personally

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.480
worked with in the past, they're called

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.200
Prime Corporate Services. I'll leave the

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.160
link down in the description below, or

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.200
you can go to talk to prime.com, and

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.120
they would be happy to have a

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.840
conversation with you about what funding

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.000
might look like for a potential business

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.440
that you're trying to buy. Business

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.239
number two is an ATM route. Now, this

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.400
one I can actually speak to again from

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:25.440
personal experience because I built an

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.320
ATM business from scratch. And it

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.960
started really simple. I saw some people

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.480
online, they were doing it, and I saw

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.200
the potential, and I decided that I

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.919
wanted to figure it out. See, you buy a

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.840
machine, you find a location, you

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:37.800
negotiate a deal with the owner of that

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.160
business where they get a cut of every

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.520
transaction when your ATM gets used, and

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.840
the machine just sits there and

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.840
generates income for both you, the ATM

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.240
owner, and the business owner. Barber

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.120
shops, gas stations, and spots in areas

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.960
where people needed cash and didn't have

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.880
great options nearby is where it all

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:55.480
started for me. The pitch to business

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.480
owners is really straightforward. You're

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.240
putting money in their pocket for doing

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.919
nothing. The better the location, the

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:02.960
bigger the cut, and everything is

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:04.680
negotiable. So, we bought our machines

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.520
outright, and we scaled the route over

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.080
time. And at our peak, we had somewhere

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.760
between 75 and 100 machines actually

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.160
running. And on paper, it looked pretty

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.080
awesome. There was a lot of recurring

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.520
revenue, it was relatively passive, and

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.240
the machines were working around the

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.480
clock. But here's what nobody told me

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:21.520
about the ATM business at scale.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:23.320
Somebody has to go load all the ATMs

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.240
with cash. And when you're running that

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:27.280
many machines, you're physically driving

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.200
around with a pretty significant amount

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.720
of $20 bills on you. So, I got to a

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.880
point where I was driving around town in

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.280
a Prius with a boatload of cash, and to

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.680
be honest with you, it was starting to

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:37.520
get a little bit sketchy for me. The

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.160
next logical step was an armored

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.280
vehicle, and at that point, I kind of

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:42.480
had to ask myself if this was really the

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.520
business that I wanted to be in

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.040
long-term at scale. So, we ended up

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.320
selling all the machines, all of the

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:50.040
locations, and I ended up moving on. So,

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.200
why the heck is it on today's list?

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.280
Because at a smaller scale, before you

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.960
hit that ceiling, the ATM route is

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.760
actually one of the most straightforward

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.000
cash flowing businesses that you can

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.080
buy. You're not buying a concept or even

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.320
a brand. You're buying machines with an

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.280
established location and an existing

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.120
transaction history. The key here is

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.960
buying a route that is already placed

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.960
and already producing. You let somebody

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.880
else do all that hard work of scouting

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:15.840
the locations and negotiating all of the

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.640
deals. You come in, you take over the

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.120
machines, and you keep that ongoing

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.040
revenue. All right, number three is a

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:23.040
laundromat. And the laundromat industry

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.120
has been printing steady income for

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.960
decades, and a lot of people completely

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.040
overlook it. So, the US laundromat

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.000
industry generates approximately 5

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.240
billion in annual revenue, and that

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.680
demand is about as recession-proof as it

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.960
can possibly get. I mean, think about

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.560
it. People need clean clothes regardless

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:41.160
of what the stock market is doing.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.880
Laundromats have a first 5-year success

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.880
rate of approximately 95%,

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:47.560
which is significantly better than the

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.360
average small business out there. That

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.880
number alone should make you stop and

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.080
think. Now, here's why buying an

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.000
existing laundromat beats building one

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:57.520
from scratch. The biggest capital

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.560
expense in this business is equipment.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.080
All the washers, all of the dryers, the

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.600
water systems, the plumbing

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.440
infrastructure, then everything that you

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:06.200
need in the actual building. When you

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.080
buy an existing operation, it's already

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:10.040
done. You're stepping into a location

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.160
that's already proven with customers who

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:14.040
already have a routine of coming in. And

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.800
profit margins on this typically range

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.360
between 20 and 35%. And for a well-run

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.400
operation, that kind of margin can

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.680
translate to a very comfortable owner

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.440
income. And in some cases, well into the

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.480
six figures without you being there

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:28.320
every single day. On the valuation side,

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.080
EBITDA multiples for mid-size

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.120
laundromats range anywhere between a

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.560
three to a six X, which means that

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:36.840
you're paying roughly three to six times

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.440
what the business earns in a year. And

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.800
that is a straightforward equation to

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.720
underwrite. So, you can look at the

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.680
numbers, run all of the math, and know

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:45.400
exactly what you're getting into before

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.000
you close the deal. The key things to

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.360
look for here when you're buying are the

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.200
age and condition of all of the

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.400
equipment, the length of the lease, and

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.680
the location. Really dense residential

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.560
areas or apartment-heavy neighborhoods

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:58.800
or even around universities are going to

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.920
be typically your highest-performing

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.880
areas. Okay, number four is buying a

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:04.680
pool route. And pool routes are one of

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:06.640
the most overlooked acquisition

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.040
opportunities in the service business

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:11.080
world. And they work almost identical to

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.840
pest control. Customers pay a monthly

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:14.680
recurring fee to have their pool

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.160
maintained on a regular schedule.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.560
They're not calling you necessarily

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.320
because something went wrong. They're on

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.960
a contract and they pay every month. And

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.440
as long as the water is clean and

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.360
there's not a bunch of leaves floating

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:26.120
around in the pool, most of the people

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.640
never even think about it. And that

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:30.440
consistency is exactly what I would want

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.479
if I was buying a business. So, let me

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.440
give you a real-life example. So, my

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:36.960
grandpa has been running a pool business

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:38.440
for 25 years. My grandpa and grandma

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.120
don't have any employees. They don't

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:41.960
have an office. They don't really have

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:43.560
any overhead at all. They just have a

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:45.920
mid-size route that they've built

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.360
together and they've serviced themselves

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.520
for a really long time. Now, my

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:51.360
grandpa's now in his 80s and he's ready

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.080
to sell it. And his kids don't want it,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.160
unfortunately. So, right now he's

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:57.160
talking to other pool cleaning operators

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.440
in the area to find the right person to

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.120
take over his clientele. And that story

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.920
is playing out all over the country

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.600
right now. You have an entire generation

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:06.360
of people who built these routes over

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:08.440
decades and they're aging out of the

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:10.440
business and their kids just simply are

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.200
not interested. And that right there is

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.000
your opportunity. You're not buying a

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.600
struggling business. You're buying

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.160
something that somebody spent

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.200
potentially 20 to 25 years building with

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.320
existing customers who have been paying

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.280
month after month for many, many years.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.080
Local markets typically price pool

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:29.000
routes between six to 12 times monthly

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.480
service revenue. So, the math is

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:32.440
actually really predictable and really

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.800
easy to run. You can calculate your

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:36.680
payback period before you ever even sign

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:39.120
anything. And a well-run route with the

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:41.080
right density of accounts can generate a

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:43.080
really solid full-time income for one

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.960
